     4	
     5	# /sdd-spec — Scaffold a Feature Specification
     6	
     7	Scaffold a new Feature Specification using the SDD methodology.
     8	
     9	## Usage
    10	```
    11	/sdd-spec <feature-name> [--type feature|hotfix] [--base-branch <branch>] [-- free-form description and notes]
    12	```
    13	
    14	`--type` / `--base-branch` are explicit overrides for the flow resolved in
    15	§2d — they win over any brainstorm/proposal frontmatter and over the
    16	`WorkKind` mapping (FEAT-466). Omit both to let §2d resolve the flow from
    17	the exploration doc (or default to `feature`/`dev` when none exists).
    18	
    19	## Guardrails
    20	- Always use the official template at `sdd/templates/spec.md`.
    21	- Do NOT write implementation bodies in the spec — but every §3 module MUST
    22	  carry an **Interface Skeleton** (signatures, docstrings, `verified:` anchors;
    23	  see §4 item 6). Bodies belong to task Implementation Blueprints (`/sdd-task`).
    24	- **Feature IDs are unique by construction**: new `FEAT-<NNN>` numbers are
    25	  reserved via `scripts/sdd/reserve_ids.py` (FEAT-387) — a git-native
    26	  compare-and-swap ledger, not a manual "check existing specs" scan — so
    27	  two `/sdd-spec` runs racing each other cannot silently collide on the
    28	  same number. See §5 below. The one documented exception is intentional
    29	  `FEAT-<NNN>` reuse across a deliberate multi-spec split of one
    30	  initiative (e.g. FEAT-380 across `sandbox-hardening`,
    31	  `shelltool-hardening`, `tool-result-compression`) — declared explicitly
    32	  via a `reuse_feature_id` frontmatter field, never a silent fallback.
    33	- If a `.brainstorm.md` exists for this feature in `sdd/proposals/`, use it as input.
    34	- **NEVER re-ask a question that the brainstorm already answered.** Resolved
    35	  answers must be carried forward verbatim, not re-opened. See §2 for the
    36	  resolved-question convention and §3 for what you MAY ask.
    37	- **Always commit the spec file to the current branch** so worktrees can see it.
    38	
    39	## Steps
    40	
    41	### 1. Parse Input
    42	- **feature-name**: slug-friendly kebab-case. If not provided, ask.
    43	- **free-form notes**: anything after `--`, used as Problem Statement seed.
    44	
    45	### 2. Check for Prior Exploration (and carry it forward)
    46	
    47	Look for prior exploration documents in `sdd/proposals/`:
    48	- `<feature-name>.brainstorm.md` → structured options analysis with a Recommended Option.
    49	- `<feature-name>.proposal.md` → discussion output.
    50	
    51	If neither exists, proceed to §3.
    52	
    53	**If a brainstorm exists, you MUST treat it as the authoritative input.**
    54	Do the following in order before writing anything or asking the user anything:
    55	
    56	#### 2a. Map the brainstorm into the spec
    57	
    58	Carry each brainstorm section into the spec per this mapping. This is not
    59	optional — every non-empty brainstorm section below has a target in the spec:
    60	
    61	| Brainstorm section | Spec target |
    62	|---|---|
    63	| Problem Statement | §1 Motivation — Problem Statement (verbatim, condensed only if needed) |
    64	| Constraints & Requirements | §1 Goals + §5 Acceptance Criteria (every hard constraint becomes a checkable criterion) |
    65	| Recommendation + Recommended Option body | §2 Architectural Design — Overview |
    66	| Feature Description → User-Facing Behavior | §2 Overview |
    67	| Feature Description → Internal Behavior | §2 Component Diagram + Integration Points |
    68	| Feature Description → Edge Cases & Error Handling | §7 Known Risks / Gotchas |
    69	| Capabilities (New + Modified) | §3 Module Breakdown (one module per capability as a starting point) |
    70	| Impact & Integration table | §2 Integration Points |
    71	| Code Context (entire section) | §6 Codebase Contract (re-verify every reference — code may have shifted) |
    72	| Libraries / Tools table | §7 External Dependencies |
    73	| Parallelism Assessment | Worktree Strategy section |
    74	| Open Questions (see 2b) | §8 Open Questions (with resolved/unresolved state preserved) |
    75	
    76	Rejected options from the brainstorm are NOT carried into the spec body.
    77	They may be referenced in one line inside §1 Non-Goals if the rejection
    78	excludes something a reader might expect (e.g. *"Runtime fallback-on-failure
    79	was rejected in brainstorm — see proposals/<name>.brainstorm.md Option A."*).
